# Rust patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Rust design (Rust API Guidelines, the Rust Design Patterns book). Applies
only to `*.rs` files.

## Crate and module layout

- No shared-types-only modules ("models", "types", "common").
- A `[workspace]` only for genuinely independent crates (a library plus a
  CLI, services sharing a core crate), not a single-binary project.
- Re-export the types callers need from `lib.rs`; keep internal modules
  `pub(crate)` or private.

## Error handling design

- A library defines its own `thiserror` enum, one variant per failure mode,
  `#[source]`/`#[from]` on variants wrapping another error; no single
  `Other(String)` variant.
- An application/binary crate uses `anyhow::Result` at its outer layers and
  `.context("doing x")`/`.with_context(|| ...)` at each `?` that crosses a
  meaningful boundary.
- No `Result<T, String>`/`Result<T, Box<dyn Error>>` as a permanent library
  design; `Box<dyn Error>` is for prototypes, graduate to a `thiserror` enum.

## Ownership-driven API design

- `self` by value only when the method consumes the value (a builder's
  `.build()`), `&mut self` to mutate, `&self` otherwise.
- Builder (`FooBuilder`, chained `with_x` returning `Self`, terminal
  `.build() -> Result<Foo, ...>`) for a constructor with many optional
  parameters.
- Newtypes (`struct UserId(u64);`) over a bare primitive when the value has
  domain meaning.

## Trait design

- Define a trait at the abstraction a consumer needs, sized to the methods
  that consumer calls, not every method the concrete type has.
- Generics (`fn process<T: Processor>(p: &T)`) for hot paths where static
  dispatch matters; `Box<dyn Trait>`/`&dyn Trait` for heterogeneous
  collections or boundaries where the type varies at runtime.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A `.clone()` inserted to pass the borrow checker; flag any clone in a hot
  path with no comment explaining it.
- Stringly-typed errors (`Result<T, String>`) in a library crate.
- A "god module" (`utils`, `helpers`, `common`).
- `unwrap()`/`expect()` left in as a development shortcut when the change
  ships.
